Chapter: ValueWhen
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 A `when` in genuine VALUE position -- nested inside a larger expression, not
 as a function body -- whose branch BINDS a payload, taken and not taken in
 the same run.

 THE NESTING IS THE POINT AND THE FIXTURE IS DEAD WITHOUT IT. A `when` that
 IS a function body is lowered through the statement path, which emits a real
 `if` and assigns the binder; that path was always correct. Only a `when`
 inside a bigger expression reaches the `merge` lowering, where Fortran has no
 statement to assign a binder in. Written as a body, this fixture passes
 whether or not the defect is present.

  bump : Maybe Integer -> Integer
  bump (m) = 1 + (when m is Just (v) -> v + 100 is None -> 0)

  label : Maybe Text -> Text
  label (m) = "[" & (when m is Just (t) -> "got:" & t is None -> "none") & "]"

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni ("bump just  : " & show (bump (Just 5)))
   print-line-uni ("bump none  : " & show (bump None))
   print-line-uni ("label just : " & label (Just "abc"))
   print-line-uni ("label none : " & label None)
  end